> Gotcha.  So..... why don't we look at start times then?

Well, vanilla logfiles don't record that information, but at least for
the past year or two the tournament has used xlogfile, which does, so
that should be doable. Unless I am misunderstanding the logfile, though,
there is one major issue, which is that games not ended don't end up
in the logfile. This presents a decently serious problem, as this type
of server-scumming (as I've seen it called) is invisible. If I want a
streak, I can start games on any of eight servers, and if one is going
to die on the next turn, save it and never look back. No one will know.

But it's not as simple as just taking every unfinished game on Dec 1st,
quitting it, and then parsing the logfile; what if that character just
didn't have time to finish? What if I accidentally logged into the wrong
server to start a character? What if the server was down for two weeks
and I forgot about it?

I wasn't able to find a way to solve this problem for the CAO Crawl
tournament either; we only had two servers, on different continents, which
mitigated it significantly. I went with "small number of servers with
high capacity and reliability," and that worked out; but I think devnull
has a lot more players than we did (we had 400, but the bottom hundred
didn't actually score any points) so that might not work out so well.

So looking at start times solves the "play until five games are on the
high altar and sac them all, even if there are tons of intervening deaths"
case, but not the "can usually but not always ascend, don't let any games
die until you've run out of free servers" case. Unless you're willing
to penalize people who don't finish characters they started in the middle,
that is, which is a reasonable stance but not one I would take personally.

